      "text": "Vardaman takes its name from James Kimble Vardaman, who served as Mississippi’s governor from 1904 to 1908.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vardaman%2C_Mississippi",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Founded in 1904, Vardaman grew around a railroad line that linked Calhoun County to the wider region.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/vardaman_mississippi",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Each November, Vardaman hosts the National Sweet Potato Festival, drawing visitors to its first‑week celebrations.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        20
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vardaman%2C_Mississippi",
      "category": "culture",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Vardaman proudly claims the title 'Sweet Potato Capital of the World,' reflecting its deep agricultural roots.",
